{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>וְהִתְהַלַּכְתִּי בְּתוֹכְכֶם.</b> Rashi explained: 'I will stroll with you in the Garden of Eden,' etc. His intent is to remove from our holy Torah every claimant and objector who says, 'I have room to lodge and to argue' — for since the Torah does not mention the principal reward of the soul, if so, surely these commandments have no power to bequeath to their doers the spiritual reward of the World to Come, and the ultimate purpose of their performance is nothing but to receive reward in this world, which is gripped in cords of contempt. And indeed the perfect ones and many others have already been aroused over this doubt, and seven opinions have come to resolve this doubt; Rabbi Isaac (Maharil) recorded them in his work and treated them at length, and I have come to abbreviate as much as possible and to arrange them here in order to silence the mouth of speakers of falsehood against our holy Torah." "The first opinion is the opinion of the Rambam, that all these promises are not the principal reward, and all the evils and the good things mentioned here in this passage speak only of the matter of the removal of hindrances alone — that is to say, if you keep My commandments I will remove from you every hindrance, such as wars and diseases and famine and sorrow, so that you will be able to serve HaShem without any hindrance. But the principal reward of the World to Come is not mentioned here, so that one will serve his Creator for its own sake, and not for the sake of that reward or out of fear of punishment; see in Sefer ha-Madda (Hilkhot Teshuvah 9:1)." "The second opinion is the opinion of Rabbi Abraham Ibn Ezra, in the portion of Ha'azinu (Deuteronomy 32:39), and this is his language: 'And in my opinion the Torah was given to all and not to one alone, and the matters of the World to Come not one in a thousand will understand, for it is deep' — thus far his language. And his opinion is that since it is difficult to depict that reward, for the material being cannot grasp the spiritual matter, therefore the Torah concealed this deep matter from the multitude because of the narrowness of their understanding." "The third opinion is the opinion of Rabbeinu Bachya the Elder — the Ibn Ezra mentioned him in the portion of Ha'azinu and inclined toward his words, and the Ramban also inclined toward this opinion — and it is that all the promises in the Torah are above nature, for it is not a natural thing that rains should fall when one performs the commandments and that rain should be withheld from the earth when one does not do the will of the Holy One, blessed be He. However, that the soul ascends to the place from which it was hewn — this is a thing natural to the soul, and this is not in the way of a marvel. And since we find in the Torah the punishment of excision (karet) for the sinning soul, that it will be cut off from the place of its hewing, from this one may learn that if one does not sin one will return to her dwelling-place, to the place where her tent was at first. And it appears that concerning this it is said (Psalms 37:34), ״קַוֵּה אֶל ה׳ וִירוֹמִמְךָ לָרֶשֶׁת אָרֶץ, בְּהִכָּרֵת רְשָׁעִים תִּרְאֶה״ — that is to say: in that excision promised in the Torah to the wicked, you will see that there is spiritual reward, for HaShem will raise you up to inherit the land of the living, for the soul will ascend to the place of its hewing — as it was necessary to cut off the souls of the wicked from there." "The fourth opinion is that in those days the whole world denied the providence of HaShem, blessed be He, and they would claim that everything done in the world is all by necessity and not by will; the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to affirm the principle of providence through these promises, which their eyes see — that all who do the will of their Creator are watched over for good through all these providences. And had He promised them the spiritual reward, they would still have remained in this denial, for one who wishes to lie will distance his witnesses. And the principal of this opinion is the opinion of Rabbeinu Nissim in the portion of Bereshit, and it is rooted in Sefer ha-Kuzari (First Essay 79–80), when the Kuzari said to the Companion: 'I see that the promises of others are plumper and fatter than your promises.' The Companion answered him: 'But they are all after death, and in life there is nothing from them — not a thing that the senses testify to.' The Kuzari said: 'And I have not seen one of those who believe in those promises yearning to acquire it quickly; but if he had it in his hand to postpone them a thousand years while he remains in the remembrance of life and under the yoke of this world and the abundance of its sorrow, he would choose this.' Thus far his words. And this is indeed an unanswerable reply, to say that the Torah promised the thing which a person desires greatly." "The fifth opinion is that before the giving of the Torah they used to worship stars and constellations, and they would perform for them specific rites in order to draw down blessing from them upon the crops and to bring rain in its season and the rest of the body's successes. And when the Holy One, blessed be He, gave the Torah and withheld them from those rites, it was necessary to promise them that even through observance of the Torah they would merit those promises, and through the rites of the stars and constellations they would lack all of these. But the life of the World to Come did not need to be promised to them, for even through those rites they had no assurance in it. And this is the opinion of Rav Saadiah in his Book of Beliefs and Opinions, and also in the Guide of the Perplexed, part three." "The sixth opinion is that since it says ״וְהִתְהַלַּכְתִּי בְּתוֹכְכֶם וְנָתַתִּי מִשְׁכָּנִי בְּתוֹכְכֶם״ — which is the cleaving of the Shekhinah to Israel even in this world, where the soul is entangled with matter — how much more so shall it be cleaved to the Shekhinah after it is separated from matter. Behold, all that the counterfeit religions promised after death, the Torah promised us even in the life of this world, and the prophecy that exists among us bears witness. And this opinion you will find also in the reply of the Rabbi to the King of the Khazar at the end of essay one of his book, and Rabbeinu Nissim held this opinion as well in his novel derashot." "The seventh opinion is that all the promises mentioned in the Torah pertain to the totality of the nation, for the world is judged according to the majority, and the promise of material bounty and crops and peace and the like will be for all Israel as one. But the reward of the soul in the World to Come does not pertain to the totality of the nation; rather, each individual is judged there on his own according to his deeds. And this is alluded to in the commandments of honoring father and mother and sending away the mother bird — for each individual of Israel. And this opinion is in the Book of Principles and in the Ramban, parashat Eikev (Deuteronomy 11:13) on the verse ״בְּכָל לְבַבְכֶם״ — see there. In these seven ways, those who speak slander against our holy Torah will flee — apart from what we have seen with our own eyes, how great was the love of HaShem for our fathers Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. And had their success in this world been the end of their success, what advantage would Abraham have had over the wicked Nimrod? For the latter ruled as king, while Abraham was a wanderer all his days, journeying from tent to tent, from nation to nation; and likewise Isaac and Jacob — we would not know what came to them of true reward. For if this reward promised to their seed is the end of their reward — what profit is it to its masters, for after their death their seed inherits the land while they depart from the world empty of all their toil? And even for Israel, who inherit the land — what advantage do they have, even in the time of tranquility, over all the kings of the earth who prospered and ruled in the world like them, and some of them more than they? This is only because the abundance of the good hidden away for the righteous is the portion and inheritance of the fathers and of all their descendants like them, for one Torah applies to them all."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {4 "The Kuzari passage cited is from First Essay, discusssing temporal vs. otherworldly promises; the specific paragraph numbering (קד-קו in Hebrew letter-numerals = 104-105) may reflect a different edition's numbering from the standard 79-80."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:08.673310070Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}